<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#bull-leaping">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bull-leaping</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taurokathapsia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bull-leaping is an acrobatic feat in which a performer vaults over a charging bull, shown above all in the art of Minoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, where it is thought to have been a key ritual of the elite and perhaps of religion. Leapers, possibly both men and women, are shown grasping the horns or somersaulting over the bull's back, though whether the images record a real performance is debated. Similar scenes occur in Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the feat survives as a sport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bull-leaping.jpg" alt="Bull-leaping" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Leaping over bulls is a motif of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> figurative art, above all Minoan, and appears on what are probably Minoan objects found in Mycenaean Greece; it also occurs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>. A study by Younger in 1995 sorted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> images into three types: an acrobat who approaches from the front, grasps the horns and somersaults backwards; one who dives over the horns without touching them and pushes off the bull's back; and one shown in mid-air over the back, facing the same way as the animal. The third type is common in Late Minoan IIIB art of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE.</p><p>Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, frescoes at Tell el-Daba, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, from the 18th dynasty show similar designs; they have often been credited to Egyptian craftsmen trained by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a>, though this is disputed. A seal impression from level VII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> shows two acrobats doing handstands on a bull, and a relief vase found at Hüseyindede in 1997 dates to the Hittite Old Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, as in other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> civilisations, the bull was venerated, and its image is widespread at the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. Scholars debate whether the art records a real ritual sport in which athletes vaulted over bulls; one hypothesis holds that the leaper grasped the horns and the bull's upward jerk of the neck gave the momentum for somersaults. Barbara Olsen of Vassar College judges that the sport was probably not especially dangerous, as the images usually end well, though a goring scene on the boxer's rhyton from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Hagia Triada</a> suggests that injuries occurred.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Related contests survive. In south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the course landaise, held from March to October at festivals in Nogaro, Mont-de-Marsan, Dax and elsewhere, pits teams of leapers and dodgers against cows guided by a rope on the horns; it is dangerous for the people, and the competitor Jean-Pierre Rachou was killed in 2001. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recortadores dodge and leap over unrestrained bulls, some with a pole. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, jallikattu is held at Pongal; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Supreme Court banned it several times after deaths and injuries, and an ordinance of 2017 allowed it to continue after protests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bull-leaping” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bull-leaping</b>, shown in wall paintings, on seals and in bronze, in which young athletes grasp or vault over a charging bull, may have been a ritual, a sport or both.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Knossos was the centre of Minoan civilisation, with frescoes such as the <b>bull-leaping</b> scene, light wells, storerooms of pithoi and a theatral area.</blockquote><a href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Palace State of Knossos</a></li></ul></section>
